{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Lest there be among you, etc., to go to serve, etc.</b> Here the verse warns concerning the thought of idolatrous worship, as it warns by saying ״lest״ regarding one whose heart turns to go to serve, etc." "<b>Lest there be among you, etc., a root, etc.</b> One needs to know what is this root that bears gall and wormwood — if it refers back to ״whose heart turns, etc.״ said just above, he should not have said ״lest there be among you״ a second time; rather, he should have said it this way: ״Lest there be among you a man, etc., a root bearing gall and wormwood, whose heart,״ etc. Further, one needs to know the intent of the words themselves of <b>a root bearing</b>, etc., for it appears that it does not bear gall and wormwood, but rather [it is merely] a root. Further, one needs to know in what this blessing — that this root blesses itself in his heart — consists of, how it blesses itself. Further, it says ״saying.״ Further, it gives this reason where it says ״for in the stubbornness,״ etc., and what does it mean by ״in order,״ etc.?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is that a person should not think in his mind to lighten upon himself the yoke of punishment, and suppose in his mind that he does no wrong in this, but rather applies his intellect so as not to incur all the curses. He would never think in his mind to transgress the word of HaShem, great or small. Corresponding to this, the verse says <b>lest there be among you a root bearing gall and wormwood</b> — meaning that at present it is only a root, and it informs that from it will sprout gall and wormwood, as I shall explain." "And the verse explained what the root is, and said: ״And it shall come to pass, when he hears the words of the imprecation״ — <b>and it shall come to pass</b> is an expression of joy, for he rejoices upon hearing that the thing's obligation stems only from the imprecation, which indicates that without the imprecation he would not be liable. <b>And he blesses himself in his heart</b> — he trusts that the curse will not come upon him because of his heart, in this manner. <b>Saying</b> — meaning, he will say: peace shall be for me, for in the stubbornness, etc.; meaning, even though Moses administers an oath to them and he answers Amen, he nullifies it in his heart. And the essence of an oath is the heart, as in the words of Rabbi Akiva who said (Shevuot 26a): ״a person in an oath״ — that his heart and his mouth must be equal. And whoever nullifies it in his heart, even though he utters it with his lips, we follow only the intention of the heart. And that is what it says: <b>for in the stubbornness of my heart I go</b> — for the essential thing on which the matter of the oath depends is the heart, and therefore it called it stubbornness. And in that he does not accept the oath in his heart, he does not incur the entire punishment stated — whether upon himself, he is liable only for the punishment of a single transgression, as we have written; whether upon the children — his sons, he does not obligate them through the force of his oath; whether in the matter of suretyship for others. And that is what it says: <b>in order to add the swept-away to the thirsty</b> — meaning, ״swept-away (רוה)״ is an expression denoting multitude, and ״thirsty (צמאה)״ is an expression denoting paucity. And the intent is that he is clever to cut down the multitude of punishment to make it paucity, by nullifying the oath in his heart." "<b>And this thing</b> Moses called a ״root,״ for from it will sprout gall and wormwood. The reason is that the very essence of the purpose of the oath is so that through it one may strengthen himself against the inciter to sin, as it says (Psalms 119:106): ״I have sworn and I will fulfill it,״ and like the deed of Boaz, who swore to his evil inclination (Numbers Rabbah 15:16). Therefore a person who thinks this thought is being seduced by the aspect of evil, which plants within him an evil root, so that afterward it becomes easy to plant within his innards a root of wickedness to darken his deeds. And it says to him: is the matter really dependent on anything but the oath? You did not swear — arise, make yourself gods, and do whatever you desire to do." "<b>And He called</b> the evil deed ״רֹאשׁ וְלַעֲנָה״ (gall and wormwood), because the taste of transgressions is רֹאשׁ וְלַעֲנָה. This is the sod of the bitter drop in the sword of the angel of death (עבודה זרה כ:), which comes into being through a person's transgressions, in the sod of ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״ (Jeremiah 2:19). And the opposite holds for a good deed, in the sod of ״טַעֲמוּ וּרְאוּ כִּי טוֹב ה׳״ (Psalms 34:9)." "<b>And the verse singles out</b> a punishment for this one beyond the rest of those who enter the covenant, saying ״לֹא יֹאבֶה״ etc. — meaning: even though HaShem's attribute is to pardon and forgive the iniquities of His people Israel when they appease Him, as is known, yet for this man — ״לֹא יֹאבֶה ה׳״. In using the expression ״לֹא יֹאבֶה״, the intent is that HaShem will reverse within him His will that pardons, and He will not be willing to forgive. And the consequence of HaShem's not being willing to forgive him is ״כִּי אָז יֶעְשַׁן אַף ה׳״. The word ״אָז״ means: when afterward he again sins by producing רֹאשׁ וְלַעֲנָה — then, instead of what he thought would lighten his punishment, the opposite occurs: the anger of HaShem will smoke, and His jealousy will burn against that man, and the entire curse written in this book will lie upon him." "<b>Thus it turns out</b> that not only did he gain nothing from his evil thought — for all the evil will befall him — but further, the entire curse will lie upon him. This would not be so if he accepts the oath now: for even though he becomes liable to it when evil comes upon him, he may call out to HaShem and He will forgive him, and the entire curse will not lie upon him, since he did what was incumbent upon him and accepted the curse now; he merely struggled against his evil inclination — may his Maker have mercy on him. Not so the man of Belial who nullifies the oath now in his heart, for he hardens himself against knowing, and brings evil upon himself." "<b>Now behold,</b> although the verse speaks of ״that man,״ the same applies to a family or a tribe, just as the verse reveals at the end, as it says (Deuteronomy 29:21): ״וְאָמַר הַדּוֹר הָאַחֲרוֹן״ etc. ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ הַהִיא״ etc. — see, then, that the stated punishment was singled out not for one man alone but also for the many who plan such a thing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:35.709525198Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}